.TH grapheme n 9.2 Tcl "Tcl Built-In Commands"
.so man.macros
.BS
'\" Note:  do not modify the .SH NAME line immediately below!
.SH NAME
grapheme \- Operations on extended grapheme clusters
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
\fBgrapheme count \fIstring\fR
\fBgrapheme index \fIstring grIndex\fR
\fBgrapheme next \fIstring indexVar\fR
\fBgrapheme offset \fIstring grIndex\fR
\fBgrapheme prev \fIstring indexVar\fR
\fBgrapheme range \fIstring grFirst grLast\fR
\fBgrapheme reverse \fIstring\fR
\fBgrapheme split \fIstring\fR
.fi
.BE
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
The \fBgrapheme\fR command operates on the \fIextended grapheme clusters\fR in
a string, henceforth referred to simply as \fIgraphemes\fR. A grapheme is what
a user perceives as a single character, and may consist of more than one
Unicode code point. For example, a base letter followed by a combining accent,
a CR LF sequence, or a multi-person emoji sequence joined by zero width
joiners are each a single grapheme. See Unicode Standard Annex #29 (UAX #29)
for a formal definition.
.PP
The \fBgrapheme\fR operations are analogous to those of the \fBstring\fR
command but operate on graphemes instead of code points. They are more
suitable in cases where the result should match the user's perception of
a string, such as when computing displayed lengths, cursor movement, or
text operations such as wrapping or reversing.
.PP
Several operations take a \fIgrapheme index\fR, which refers to the position
of a grapheme within a \fIstring\fR counted in graphemes. Grapheme indices may
be specified in the same forms accepted by the \fIindex\fR argument of the
\fBstring index\fR command, including \fBend\fR, which denotes the index of
the last grapheme in the string, and simple expressions.
.PP
The following operations are supported by the command:
.\" METHOD: count
.TP
\fBgrapheme count \fIstring\fR
.
Returns the number of graphemes in \fIstring\fR.
.\" METHOD: index
.TP
\fBgrapheme index \fIstring grIndex\fR
.
Returns the grapheme at grapheme index \fIgrIndex\fR in \fIstring\fR. If
\fIgrIndex\fR is out of bounds, an empty string is returned.
.\" METHOD: next
.TP
\fBgrapheme next \fIstring indexVar\fR
.
Returns the grapheme in \fIstring\fR that starts at the code point index
stored in the variable \fIindexVar\fR. The value of \fIindexVar\fR may be in
any of the forms accepted by the \fBstring index\fR command. If
\fIindexVar\fR is not defined or its value is negative, the index is taken
as 0. If the index is greater than the number of code points in
\fIstring\fR, an empty string is returned.
.RS
.PP
When the command returns, \fIindexVar\fR holds the code point index following
the returned grapheme, or, if the empty string was returned, the length of
\fIstring\fR in code points. The command can thus be called repeatedly to
iterate over the graphemes in a string.
.RE
.\" METHOD: offset
.TP
\fBgrapheme offset \fIstring grIndex\fR
.
Maps the grapheme index \fIgrIndex\fR in \fIstring\fR to the corresponding
code point index. Returns \-1 if \fIgrIndex\fR is out of bounds.
.\" METHOD: prev
.TP
\fBgrapheme prev \fIstring indexVar\fR
.
Returns the grapheme in \fIstring\fR that precedes the code point index stored
in the variable \fIindexVar\fR. The value of \fIindexVar\fR may be in any of
the forms accepted by the \fBstring index\fR command. If \fIindexVar\fR is
not defined, it defaults to the length of \fIstring\fR in code points, so
that the last grapheme in the string is returned. If the value is negative,
it is taken as 0 and an empty string is returned. If it is greater than the
number of code points in \fIstring\fR, it is taken as the length of
\fIstring\fR in code points.
.RS
.PP
When the command returns a non-empty string, \fIindexVar\fR holds the code
point index of the returned grapheme. If the command returns an empty string,
\fIindexVar\fR is set to 0. The command can thus be called repeatedly to
iterate backwards over the graphemes in a string.
.RE
.\" METHOD: range
.TP
\fBgrapheme range \fIstring grFirst grLast\fR
.
Returns the substring of \fIstring\fR between the grapheme indices
\fIgrFirst\fR and \fIgrLast\fR, both inclusive. If \fIgrFirst\fR is negative,
it is treated as 0. If \fIgrLast\fR is greater than or equal to the number of
graphemes in \fIstring\fR, it is treated as the index of the last grapheme.
If \fIgrLast\fR is less than \fIgrFirst\fR, an empty string is returned.
.\" METHOD: reverse
.TP
\fBgrapheme reverse \fIstring\fR
.
Returns a string comprising the graphemes of \fIstring\fR in reverse order.
.\" METHOD: split
.TP
\fBgrapheme split \fIstring\fR
.
Returns a list whose elements are the graphemes that comprise \fIstring\fR.
.SH "PERFORMANCE NOTES"
.PP
Random access using grapheme indices requires the string to be scanned from
the beginning and is therefore expensive. When iterating over the graphemes
of a string, use \fBgrapheme next\fR and \fBgrapheme prev\fR, or use
\fBforeach\fR in conjunction with \fBgrapheme split\fR, in preference to a
loop that uses indices.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Iterating forwards over the graphemes of a string:
.PP
.CS
set pos 0; # Or leave undefined
while {[set gr [grapheme next $s pos]] ne ""} {
    puts $gr
}
.CE
.PP
Or alternatively,
.PP
.CS
foreach gr [grapheme split $s] {
    puts $gr
}
.CE
.PP
Iterating backwards over the graphemes of a string.
.PP
.CS
set pos end; # Or leave undefined
while {[set gr [grapheme prev $s pos]] ne ""} {
    puts $gr
}
.CE
.SH "SEE ALSO"
string(n), Tcl_Grapheme(3)
.SH KEYWORDS
extended grapheme cluster, grapheme, string, Unicode
